File: PHCpack.info, Node: toLaurentPolynomial, Next: topWitnessSet, Prev: tDegree, Up: Top

toLaurentPolynomial -- converts a list of rational polynomials into Laurent polynomials
***************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        toLaurentPolynomial(system, w)
  * Inputs:
      * system, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of rational
        polynomials from the same ring
      * w, a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, a symbol to be used for new
        indexed variables
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, the same system converted to a
        Laurent polynomial system

Description
===========



This function converts a rational system to a Laurent polynomial system.



+--------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : QQ[x,y,z];                                   |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : sys = {y-x^2, z-x^3, (x+y+z-1)/x};           |
+--------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : describe ring ideal sys                      |
|                                                  |
|o3 = frac(QQ[x..z, Degrees => {3:1}, Heft => {1}])|
+--------------------------------------------------+



There are denominators, so the method will convert these:



+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : convertedSys = toLaurentPolynomial(sys,w);|
+-----------------------------------------------+



The system convertedSys is an equivalent system living in a Laurent polynomial
ring. For each denominator, a new variable was created starting with w.



+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : printWidth = 300;                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : toString convertedSys                                            |
|                                                                      |
|o6 = {y-x^2, z-x^3, -w_0^(-1)+z*w_0^(-1)+y*w_0^(-1)+x*w_0^(-1), w_0-x}|
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : ring ideal convertedSys                                          |
|                                                                      |
|o7 = QQ {x..z, w }                                                    |
|                0                                                     |
|                                                                      |
|o7 : PolynomialRing                                                   |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+



Next, notice that the option "Inverses=>true" shows that this is a ring with
negative exponents allowed:



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : describe oo                                                             |
|                                                                             |
|o8 = QQ {x..z, w , Degrees => {4:1}, MonomialOrder => {Weights => {4:-1} },  |
|                0                                     {MonomialSize => 32}   |
|                                                      {GroupRevLex => 4  }   |
|                                                      {Position => Up    }   |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Inverses => true, Local => true, Global => false}                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+



Note that if the system is already polynomial, or in Laurent polynomial form,
then toLaurentPolynomial doesn't change it. Of course, sometimes it is possible
that the system is polynomial "in disguise" as in line o10 in the following
example:



+---------------------------+
|i9 : P = QQ[x,y,z];        |
+---------------------------+
|i10 : f = (x*y + z^2*y) / y|
|                           |
|       2                   |
|o10 = z  + x               |
|                           |
|o10 : frac P               |
+---------------------------+
|i11 : liftable(f,P)        |
|                           |
|o11 = true                 |
+---------------------------+



But toLaurentPolynomial detects this and simplifies the system. Instead of
creating Laurent polynomials, it updates the system using the following:



+---------------+
|i12 : lift(f,P)|
|               |
|       2       |
|o12 = z  + x   |
|               |
|o12 : P        |
+---------------+

Ways to use toLaurentPolynomial:
================================

  * "toLaurentPolynomial(List,Symbol)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note toLaurentPolynomial: toLaurentPolynomial, is a *note method
function: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

