zeroFilter -- returns solutions with k-th coordinate less than the given tolerance
**********************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        zeroSols = zeroFilter(sols,k,tol)
  * Inputs:
      * sols, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, containing the solutions of a
        polynomial system
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, index of a coordinate of a
        solution
      * tol, a *note real number: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,, tolerance on the absolute
        value of the k-th coordinate
  * Outputs:
      * zeroSols, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, solutions with k-th
        coordinate less than the given tolerance

Description
===========



A solution has its k-th coordinate zero when the abs function evaluates to a
number less than or equal to the given tolerance.



+-------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = CC[x,y];                                |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = { x^3*y^5 + y^2 + x^2*y, x*y + x^2 - 1};|
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : fSols = solveSystem(f, randomSeed=>3);      |
+-------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : fSols/print                                 |
|{-1, 0}                                          |
|{1, 0}                                           |
|{-.894935+.624334*ii, .143333-1.14868*ii}        |
|{-.764107, -.544612}                             |
|{.742585+.425943*ii, .270685-1.00715*ii}         |
|{-.894935-.624334*ii, .143333+1.14868*ii}        |
|{1.33076-.335184*ii, -.62414+.513163*ii}         |
|{.742585-.425943*ii, .270685+1.00715*ii}         |
|{-1.59272, .964857}                              |
|{1.33076+.335184*ii, -.62414-.513163*ii}         |
|                                                 |
|o4 = {, , , , , , , , , }                        |
|                                                 |
|o4 : List                                        |
+-------------------------------------------------+



There is one solution with zero second coordinate:



+--------------------------------------------+
|i5 : zeroSols = zeroFilter(fSols,1,1.0e-10);|
+--------------------------------------------+
|i6 : zeroSols / print                       |
|{-1, 0}                                     |
|{1, 0}                                      |
|                                            |
|o6 = {, }                                   |
|                                            |
|o6 : List                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+



Here is another system where we filter solutions with `small' first coordinate:



+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : f = {x^2+y^2,y*x+x};                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : fSols = solveSystem(f);                             |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : fSols/print                                         |
|(6.16298e-33-1.54074e-33*ii, -1.37536e-32+1.22052e-32*ii)|
|{ii, -1}                                                 |
|{-ii, -1}                                                |
|                                                         |
|o9 = {, , }                                              |
|                                                         |
|o9 : List                                                |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : zeroSols = zeroFilter(fSols,0,1.0e-10);            |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : zeroSols/print                                     |
|(6.16298e-33-1.54074e-33*ii, -1.37536e-32+1.22052e-32*ii)|
|                                                         |
|o11 = {}                                                 |
|                                                         |
|o11 : List                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------+



Good values for the tolerance are relative to the accuracy and the condition
number of the solution. To improve the accuracy of a solution, apply *note
refineSolutions: refineSolutions, with a higher working precision.

See also
========

  * *note refineSolutions: refineSolutions, -- refines solutions of a system by
    increasing working precision
  * *note nonZeroFilter: nonZeroFilter, -- returns solutions with coordinate
    larger than given tolerance

Ways to use zeroFilter:
=======================

  * "zeroFilter(List,ZZ,RR)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note zeroFilter: zeroFilter, is a *note method function:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunction,.

